   "answer": "Based on the lecture material, a **hazard** is defined as any condition that causes the pipeline to stall, resulting in \"much bubbles and degraded efficiency\" [S1, p. 18]. The three types of hazards differ based on the specific cause of the stall:\n\n1.  **Data Hazard**: This occurs due to **dependencies of some data**. The lecture specifies three sub-types of data dependencies:\n    *   Read-after-write (RAW)\n    *   Write-after-write (WAW)\n    *   Write-after-read (WAR) [S1, p. 18].\n\n2.  **Control Hazard**: This occurs due to **branch and synchronization** issues. The lecture notes that a deeper pipeline can cause a worse penalty in this case [S1, p. 18; S2, p. 20].\n\n3.  **Structural Hazard**: This occurs due to **contention on hardware**. Specifically, it involves competition for resources such as the memory channel, cache slot, or queue [S1, p. 18].\n\nIn summary, data hazards stem from instruction dependencies on data values, control hazards stem from the flow of control (branches/sync), and structural hazards stem from physical hardware resource limitations [S1, p. 18].",
